# Text aliases: short key -> the exact string you can never remember the spelling of.
#
# Two consumers, one list:
#   * bash sources this file (run.sh appends the line to .bashrc), so `$key`
#     expands anywhere on the command line. Deliberately NOT exported -- these
#     are typing aids for an interactive shell, not environment for every child.
#   * nvim/luasnippets/all.lua reads it and emits one snippet per key, active in
#     every filetype. Type the key, press <CR> to accept from the blink menu.
#
# Format is a bash assignment, because that is the one key=value shape that is
# both broadly readable and directly executable:
#
#     key='value'
#
# Quote the value. Unquoted works when there is nothing to quote, and a leading
# `export ` is tolerated so a line lifted from the machines/<host>.sh path
# registry pastes in unchanged. `#` starts a comment. On a duplicate key the
# last one wins -- bash's rule, and all.lua matches it.
#
# No spaces around the `=`: bash reads `k = v` as the command `k` and `k= v` as
# an empty assignment plus the command `v`. all.lua rejects both for the same
# reason, so a line can never work in nvim while failing in the shell.
#
# Prefer single quotes. bash expands `$var` and escapes inside "..." while
# all.lua takes them literally, so double quotes are the one place the two
# readers can disagree. `sa` only ever writes single quotes.
#
# `sa <key> <value>` (in .functions.sh) appends or updates an entry without
# opening this file, and sets it in the calling shell at the same time. `sl`
# lists what is registered; `srm <key>` deletes an entry (every line for that
# key, so a hand-edited duplicate goes too) and unsets it in the calling shell.
#
# After editing: `<leader>rs` in nvim (:ReloadSnippets) and a new shell (or
# `source ~/.snippet_aliases`) for bash. No installer re-run needed.

